      "answer": "Based on the current lecture, here is how SRAM and DRAM differ in data storage and their typical uses:\n\n## How They Store Data\n\n**SRAM (Static RAM)**\n- Stores data via a **stable transistor circuit** (a bistable latch).\n- Requires **6 transistors per bit**, making it \"expansive\" (larger area per bit).\n- Needs a small amount of power to retain the bit (e.g., 423 mV for a 45 nm process).\n- Reading is **non-destructive**: the cell pulls one of the bit lines, and a sense amplifier on the column detects the difference between `bit` and `bitbar` (precharged to Vdd). No rewrite is needed after a read. See page 31 [S1] and page 32 [S3].\n\n**DRAM (Dynamic RAM)**\n- Stores data as **tiny capacitor charges** — a bit is represented as charged vs. discharged.\n- Uses only **one transistor per bit**, making it cheap and dense.\n- The stored charge is weak, so the sense amplifier must detect and amplify a very small voltage change into a full 0/1 value.\n- Reading is **destructive**: reading disturbs the stored charge, so the data must be **re-written after every row activation**.\n- Because the capacitor leaks, DRAM must also be **periodically refreshed** (each row can be refreshed simultaneously). See page 32 [S3] and page 35 [S2].\n- Address lines are multiplexed: the upper half of the address is the **row access strobe (RAS)** and the lower half is the **column access strobe (CAS)**. See page 32 [S3].\n\n## Typical Uses\n\n- **SRAM:** used for **caches and register files** (fast, on-chip memory). See page 32 [S3].\n- **DRAM:** used for main memory technologies such as **DDR / GDDR / HBM**. See page 32 [S3].\n\n## Why DRAM Is Slower (context)\nThe lecture notes that DRAM is slow because of its weak storage signal, the time needed to sense/amplify the small voltage change, the destructive read requiring restore, and large dense arrays with long bitlines that have higher RC delay. See page 35 [S2].\n\n**Summary of the key contrast:** SRAM uses a 6-transistor stable circuit (non-destructive read, no refresh) and is used for caches/register files; DRAM uses a 1-transistor + capacitor cell (destructive read, periodic refresh) and is used for main memory (DDR/GDDR/HBM). [S3]",
